    {
      "parameters": {
        "mode": "runOnceForEachItem",
        "jsCode": "// Turn whatever failed into one readable sentence for the caller.\n// Instagram's Graph API buries the useful part in error.error.message, so dig\n// for it before falling back to anything else we can find.\nconst item = $input.item.json || {};\nconst err = item.error || item;\n\nlet message =\n  (err && err.error && err.error.message) ||   // Graph API shape\n  (err && err.message) ||\n  (typeof err === 'string' ? err : null) ||\n  item.reason ||\n  null;\n\nif (!message) {\n  try { message = JSON.stringify(err).slice(0, 400); } catch (e) { message = 'Unknown publish error'; }\n}\n\n// Graph API error subcodes are the difference between \"fix the image\" and\n// \"reconnect the account\", so keep them when present.\nconst code = (err && err.error && (err.error.code || err.error.error_subcode)) || null;\nconst node = $execution?.customData?.failedNode || null;\n\nreturn { json: {\n  success: false,\n  error: String(message).slice(0, 500),\n  code: code,\n  failed_node: node,\n} };"
      },
      "name": "Prepare Error",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.code",
      "typeVersion": 2,
      "position": [
        1200,
        1180
      ],
      "notesInFlow": true,
      "id": "b3prep0error0code00000000000000",
      "notes": "Normalizes any failure in the publish chain into { success:false, error }. Digs out the Graph API's error.error.message, which is where the actionable text actually lives."
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "respondWith": "json",
        "responseBody": "={{ JSON.stringify({ success: false, error: $json.error, code: $json.code || null }) }}",
        "options": {
          "responseCode": 200
        }
      },
      "name": "Error Response",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.respondToWebhook",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        1440,
        1180
      ],
      "notesInFlow": true,
      "id": "b3error0respond0node00000000000",
      "notes": "Responds 200 with success:false ON PURPOSE. Callers (the scheduler and publishToSocial in the app) read the body for the real reason; a bare non-200 would collapse every distinct Instagram failure into 'Webhook returned 500'. Without this node ANY failure returned an empty 200 body and the caller had nothing to report."
    }
